 Reproduction 
Ginkgo biloba is dioecious, with separate sexes, some trees being female and others being male. Male plants produce small pollen cones with sporophylls, each bearing two microsporangia spirally arranged around a central axis. Sex conversion, wherein certain branches of a tree change sexes, has been observed.{{r|nagata2016}} This phenomenon is difficult to research because of its rarity, as well as the practice of grafting female branches onto otherwise male trees that was common in 19th century Europe.{{r|osgf2017}}
Female plants do not produce cones. Two ovules are formed at the end of a stalk.
